DP  - 2015 Jul 16
TI  - Streetlight-Moth Interactions in Meadows Bordering Rural Roads Lit Since the
      1970s.
PG  - 220-31
LID - 10.5555/EcoLa.1409547 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Moths are among the most important pollinators of meadow plants at night, and
      artificial light draws them away from flowers. METHODS: We conducted two
      paired, replicated, observer-blind, field surveys (LANTERN and BEACON) that were
      designed to assess the effects of streetlights in combination with road traffic
      on moths in meadows 12 metres or closer to a road that had been lit since the
      1970s. CONCLUSIONS: These data show that streetlights in combination with
      traffic reduced the visits of moths to flowers in meadows bordering the
      road. (Funded by the Brisbane Field Naturalists and others; LANTERN and
      BEACON survey numbers, S-01807923 and S-01807949.)
